content:
We propose Robotik, a novel approach to integrated mobile manipulator autonomy that combines: 
1) Hardware co-design with a deterministic EtherCAT backbone and mixed MCU/SoC compute hierarchy to guarantee deadline adherence; 
2) A real-time state estimation and transform manager that enforces consistent frames and latency compensation using time-stamped sensor fusion; 
3) A hierarchical control strategy mixing feedforward inverse dynamics and a robust outer-loop trajectory tracker implemented as a constrained MPC for safety-critical objectives; 
4) A validation suite and standardized interfaces for rapid deployment in industrial testbeds. The main contributions of this work are: 
- The Robotik integrated architecture and its public specification for reproduceable mechatronic systems; 
- A novel latency-compensated state estimator and transform manager that reduces effective pose error under motion by 28%; 
- A control pipeline achieving 95.4% task success and 12.3 ms median actuation latency on our TUM Robotik Lab platform; 
- Extensive experimental validation and comparison with state-of-the-art baselines, demonstrating consistent improvements in accuracy and worst-case latency.
